 The behavior you're describing - needing to click on the parent menu first and then on the submenu item - typically happens when the dropdown menu is controlled by a toggle mechanism rather than the hover state.

In Bulma, there are two ways to handle dropdowns:

1. Hover-based dropdowns using the `is-hoverable` class
2. Click-based dropdowns using the `is-active` class toggled by JavaScript

If you're experiencing this issue, here are the most likely causes and solutions:

### Issue 1: `is-hoverable` class isn't working properly

Bulma's hover-based dropdowns rely on CSS `:hover` selectors. If these aren't working, it could be due to:

- Browser extensions in Brave blocking hover functionality
- CSS conflicts overriding Bulma's hover styles
- Missing the `is-hoverable` class on dropdown items

### Solution:

Make sure your dropdowns have the `is-hoverable` class:

```html
<div class="navbar-item has-dropdown is-hoverable">
  <!-- Dropdown content -->
</div>
```

### Issue 2: CSS is being applied but JavaScript is overriding it

Sometimes JavaScript event handlers can interfere with the hover behavior.

### Solution:

Try a simplified dropdown structure with no extra JavaScript for dropdowns:

```html
<div class="navbar-item has-dropdown is-hoverable">
  <a class="navbar-link">
    Dropdown Menu
  </a>
  <div class="navbar-dropdown">
    <a class="navbar-item" href="/page1">Item 1</a>
    <a class="navbar-item" href="/page2">Item 2</a>
  </div>
</div>
```

And only include the basic hamburger menu JavaScript:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  // Get all "navbar-burger" elements
  const $navbarBurgers = Array.prototype.slice.call(document.querySelectorAll('.navbar-burger'), 0);

  // Add a click event on each of them
  $navbarBurgers.forEach(el => {
    el.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const target = el.dataset.target;
      const $target = document.getElementById(target);
      el.classList.toggle('is-active');
      $target.classList.toggle('is-active');
    });
  });
});
```

### Issue 3: Mobile behavior interfering with desktop behavior

Sometimes the mobile dropdown behavior interferes with the desktop behavior.

### Solution:

Make sure your JavaScript only applies the click behavior on mobile screen sizes:

```javascript
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  // Hamburger menu code here...

  // Only apply click behavior on mobile
  const isMobile = window.matchMedia('(max-width: 1023px)');

  if (isMobile.matches) {
    // Mobile dropdown code here...
  }
});
```

If you're still having issues, it would be helpful to see your complete navbar code including both HTML and JavaScript to pinpoint exactly what's causing the issue.

